### `[🟡 Medium]` cli/main.go:735-747 — A failed issue create is permanently skipped on later iterations
`seenFindings[key]` is set before `createIssue(...)` succeeds, so any transient GitHub/API failure marks the finding as "already tracked" even though no issue was created. In loop mode that prevents the same unresolved finding from ever being retried on the next iteration.

```diff
- if seenFindings[key] {
- 	continue
- }
- seenFindings[key] = true
- num, err := createIssue(repoRoot, f)
- if err != nil {
- 	fmt.Fprintf(os.Stderr, "  Failed to create issue for %q: %v\n", f.Title, err)
- 	continue
- }
+ if seenFindings[key] {
+ 	continue
+ }
+ num, err := createIssue(repoRoot, f)
+ if err != nil {
+ 	fmt.Fprintf(os.Stderr, "  Failed to create issue for %q: %v\n", f.Title, err)
+ 	continue
+ }
+ seenFindings[key] = true
```

**AI agent fix prompt:** Move the `seenFindings` insertion to after a successful `createIssue` call, so transient API failures do not poison the dedupe cache. Keep the existing logging and `openIssues` append behavior unchanged, but only mark a finding as seen once GitHub has actually returned an issue number.

### `[🟠 High]` cli/main.go:730-747 — Reruns can recreate issues that were already open before this process started
The previous path queried GitHub via `existingIssues()` before creating new issues; this commit removes that lookup and relies only on `seenFindings`, which is reset on every invocation. That means a fresh run with `--issues` will recreate duplicates for findings that already have matching open issues from an earlier review session.

